A||||2||theogre||Brake Fluid Choices....||||7||
Z||000000||theogre||04-28-2001||11:24 PM||||As many of you may have read in my cave.... I'm not real big on Silicone, DOT 5, brake fluid. I've mentioned "high temp" and synthetic brake fluid instead.<P>Today I got myself another bottle of fluid and read the label. (I always get either synth or high temp....) Valvoline Synthetic in this case.<P>Dry boil is over 500F and Wet boil is listed at 343. I doubt any but a total race setup could boil this stuff.<P>Wet boil of DOT 3 by comparison is about 285F.<P>That's a hell of an improvement Without the problems of converting to silicone.<P>Valvoline Synth fluid is compatible with DOT 3 and DOT 4 fluids. That means you don't have to totaly rebuild the system to use the improved fluid.<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||7
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-28-2001||11:47 PM||||So if I need to bleed my brakes, I can put in the Valvoline Synthetic to top off the resivoir without any complications?  I have only used DOT 3 stuff.  Clutch the same way?<P>And about the boil stuff, I understand that if you got your fluid boiling your in for it deep, but if I have fluid that has a higher boil over, will I notice less brake fade or something?  Like does it dissipate heat better?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-29-2001||12:15 AM||||Joe,<P>Dry Boil temp is generally an issue for hard drive and race cars.<P>Wet Boil temp means the fluid is less "damaged" by water than DOT 3.<P>Brake systems are always subject to at least some water getting in them. How the fluid reacts to this water is critical.<P><BR>If you are going to use synth fluid.... <P>plan to consume a good chunk of a quart.<P>bleed as much as you can from the brakes. purge the lines at least... there's no benefit at all using it to simply top off.<P>Yes. it is clutch compatible. The clutch uses DOT 3 normally.<P><BR>Fade is diferant from boil.<P>Fade, normally, is a gradual loss. It's caused by over heating rotors(drums) and pads(shoes).<P>Boil, nearly always, is a very sudden loss of braking. The fluid vaporises and the next time you push the pedal there is nothing.<P>Very few drivers will ever see boil problems on the road. The reason I mention this is we do have a bunch of people leaning toward DOT 5 because they are in SCCA racing. In those cases normal DOT 3 could boil. Especially if it's wet. The Synthetic Fluid eliminates most of the reasons for using DOT 5. (Wilwood and other performance brake people Do Not like DOT 5. There are links in my brake article as to why...)<P>If you can get all the bleeders open...<P>You really want to purge as much fluid as you can at least every 2 years. More often if there is any reason to think the fluid is getting wet. Always when a caliper or MC is changed. You want to do this no matter what fluid you are putting in.<P>The biggest issue for most repairs is contamination of fluid. Rust and AlOx particles in the fluid can make short work of chewing seals. Rust and AlOx are the biggest killers of seals.<P>Since the synthetic and "high temp DOT 3" fluids are close in price, it just makes sense to use the full synth fluid for major repairs. For topping off it won't matter much since the old fluid is still in most of the system.||||reg||1
